QB.add(55,[{"n":1,"q":"A 2-week-old infant presents with lethargy, poor feeding, irritability, and a bulging fontanelle. Born prematurely at 34 weeks with low birth weight, a lumbar puncture reveals cloudy cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) with high white blood cell count, decreased glucose, and elevated protein levels. Which is not a causative factor for this infant's condition?","o":[{"t":"Group B Streptococcus"},{"t":"Escherichia coli"},{"t":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"t":"Listeria monocytogenes"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"While Streptococcus pneumoniae is a significant cause of bacterial meningitis in children and adults, it is less common in neonates. Given the infant's age, prematurity, and other clinical factors, it is less likely to be the causative organism."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bacterial meningitis in neonates and children"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Causes by Age Group"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neonates Pre-school Children Older Children"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Escherichia coli Haemophilus influenzae type b Neisseria (gram-negative bacilli) Neisseria meningitidis meningitidis Group B streptococci Streptococcus pneumoniae Streptococcus Listeria monocytogenes pneumoniae (Option A, B and D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Lethargy, fever, hypothermia,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"seizure, headache, stiff neck, irritability, drowsiness, and vomiting."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnosis Lumbar puncture (CSF analysis) :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cloudy CSF with high white blood cell count (neutrophils) Elevated protein, decreased glucose Gram stain or culture reveals bacteria"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Empirical antibiotics :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Broad-spectrum antibiotics started immediately Targeted antibiotics : Based on causative bacteria Adjunctive glucocorticoid therapy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Complications Hearing loss (common with H."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"influenzae and S. pneumoniae), seizures, memory impairment, decreased intellectual function, hydrocephalus, sepsis, and risk of death"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevention Vaccination : Against H. influenzae"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"type b, N. meningitidis Chemoprophylaxis : Prophylactic antibiotics for close contacts of meningococcal meningitis cases."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency with fever, severe headache, neck stiffness, and confusion. A lumbar puncture is performed, revealing a high white blood cell count with a predominance of neutrophils and elevated protein levels. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Administer oral antibiotics"},{"t":"Initiate intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics"},{"t":"Start antitubercular therapy"},{"t":"Conduct a follow-up lumbar puncture"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical features and lumbar puncture findings of high WBC, with the predominance of neutrophils and elevated protein point to bacterial meningitis . Immediate initiation of intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics is essential due to high mortality."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bacterial Meningitis in Adults and Prevention"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitides"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"are the primary causes. Listeria monocytogenes is a significant risk in adults over 50, especially in those with diabetes, alcoholism, or weakened immune systems."},{"k":"img","v":8215},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiological Process"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Immediate Management : Start with intravenous"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"broad-spectrum antibiotics to cover pathogens. Treatment is then changed based on the identified organism and its susceptibility. (Option B) Adjunctive Therapy : Dexamethasone may be given to reduce inflammation and prevent complications, particularly if administered early. Supportive Care : Manage complications like raised intracranial pressure and seizures."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevention Vaccines for H. influenzae type b, N. meningitidis, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"S. pneumoniae. No vaccine currently exists for group B meningococcal infections. Chemoprophylaxis : Close contacts of individuals with meningococcal meningitis should receive prophylactic antibiotics like rifampicin, ciprofloxacin, azithromycin or ceftriaxone to reduce the risk of secondary infections. Rifampicin - 600 mg 12 hourly for 2 days Ciprofloxacin - 500 mg single dose Azithromycin 500 mg single dose Ceftriaxone - 250 mg Intramuscular single dose"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administer oral antibiotics (Option A): Administering oral antibiotics is inappropriate for bacterial meningitis. This condition needs rapid action due to the severity and potential complications associated with the infection."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Start antitubercular therapy (Option C) : While tubercular meningitis can cause elevated white blood cell counts and protein levels, it is not the most likely diagnosis given the clinical presentation (acute fever, severe headache, neck stiffness) and predominance of neutrophils in the CSF. Tubercular meningitis typically causes a more insidious onset and a predominance of lymphocytes in the CSF."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Conduct a follow-up lumbar puncture (Option D): Performing a follow-up lumbar puncture without starting treatment would delay necessary care and increase the risk of complications associated with untreated bacterial meningitis."}]},{"n":3,"q":"Which of the following accurately describes the CSF findings in viral encephalitis caused by HSV-1?","o":[{"t":"Predominance of neutrophils with low glucose levels"},{"t":"Lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose levels"},{"t":"High protein levels and no change in white blood cells"},{"t":"Presence of eosinophils with normal glucose levels"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In cases of viral encephalitis , particularly caused by HSV-1 , the CSF usually demonstrates an increased number of lymphocytes , indicating a viral infection. Glucose levels remain normal , which helps differentiate it from bacterial meningitis, where glucose is often low."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HSV Encephalitis Diagnosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CSF Analysis PCR Neuroimaging and EEG"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Shows lymphocytic CSF PCR for HSV is highly MRI is preferred for early detection pleocytosis (increased sensitive (96%) and specific (99%) of HSV encephalitis. Characteristic lymphocytes). for confirming diagnosis. lesions appear on T2-weighted and Normal glucose levels. diffusion-weighted MRI , affecting Slightly elevated the orbitofrontal and temporal lobes. protein concentration. EEG may show periodic slow-wave"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"activity in the temporal lobes, indicative of HSV encephalitis."},{"k":"img","v":8218},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HSV Encephalitis - T2 MRI showing high signal in Temporal lobe"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Predominance of neutrophils with low glucose levels (Option A): This finding is of bacterial meningitis rather than viral encephalitis. CSF analysis shows a significant increase in neutrophils and a reduction in glucose levels in bacterial infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High protein levels and no change in white blood cells (Option C): Viral infections lead to lymphocytic pleocytosis, which is an increase in lymphocytes alongside elevated protein levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Presence of eosinophils with normal glucose levels (Option D): Eosinophils are linked to parasitic infections or allergic responses rather than viral infections like HSV-1 encephalitis."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 25 y/o male is brought to the emergency after experiencing fever, seizures, and confusion. Neurological examination reveals hemiparesis on the left side. A preliminary diagnosis of HSV encephalitis is made after an MRI shows abnormalities in the right temporal lobe. Which complication is this patient at greatest risk for if the condition is not treated promptly?","o":[{"t":"Cognitive impairment"},{"t":"Peripheral neuropathy"},{"t":"Sudden cardiac arrest"},{"t":"Respiratory failure"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"If left untreated, HSV-1 encephalitis can lead to significant cognitive decline due to damage in the brain's temporal lobes , which are crucial for memory and language functions. Permanent neurological deficits, including memory loss, difficulty with speech (aphasia), and personality changes , are long-term outcomes. Early antiviral treatment is crucial to reduce these risks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Typical clinical features of HSV-1 encephalitis include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered sensorium for more than 24 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms of brain inflammation- Fever, headache, seizures, focal neurological deficits"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cognitive, behavioural & personality changes. (Commonly misdiagnosed for personality disorders."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other associated features can also be present, such as:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary and faecal incontinence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aseptic meningitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rashes with dermatomal distribution"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Guillain-Barré syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Peripheral neuropathy (Option B): Peripheral neuropathy involves damage to the nerves affecting the extremities, which is not a complication of HSV-1 encephalitis. HSV-1 primarily affects the central nervous system, particularly the temporal lobes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sudden cardiac arrest (Option C): It is not a consequence of HSV-1 encephalitis. The disease primarily targets the brain, and complications arise from increased intracranial pressure or widespread neurological damage rather than cardiac events."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Respiratory failure (Option D): While HSV-1 encephalitis can cause severe neurological impairment, respiratory failure is not a complication."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 45 y/o male with a history of HIV infection presents to the ED with headache, fever, photophobia, and neck stiffness. His CD4 count is 80 cells/mm³. Lumbar puncture reveals an elevated opening pressure, low glucose, and elevated protein levels. India ink staining of the cerebrospinal fluid showed the following picture. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment regimen for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Fluconazole monotherapy"},{"t":"Amphotericin B and Flucytosine"},{"t":"Voriconazole monotherapy"},{"t":"Caspofungin and Fluconazole"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation is consistent with cryptococcal meningitis , as indicated by the encapsulated yeast cells on India ink staining , most commonly caused by Cryptococcus neoformans . The recommended initial treatment is a combination of amphotericin B and flucytosine for induction therapy, followed by fluconazole for consolidation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fungal meningitis:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes Cryptococcus neoformans (most common)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Candida Histoplasma Blastomyces Coccidioides Sporothrix"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Risk factors Immunocompromised patients"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Headache"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Altered mental status, lethargy Fever Neck stiffness Nausea and vomiting."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations Lumbar Puncture: Low glucose and high protein levels."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"White cell count can be normal or higher than 20 microL and have a lymphocyte predominance. India ink stain shows encapsulated yeast cells. CSF ELISA for CRAG (Cryptococcal Antigen)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment LAMB (Liposomal Amphotericin-B) and 5-Fluorocytosine (Options A, C & D (Flucytosine) for 2 weeks ruled out) Fluconazole is given subsequently for 8-10 weeks."}],"qi":[8184]},{"n":6,"q":"During a lumbar puncture, the needle passes through several anatomical layers before reaching the subarachnoid space. Which of the following is the correct sequence of structures pierced during this procedure?","o":[{"t":"Skin fi Ligamentum flavum fi Supraspinous ligament fi Interspinous ligament fi Dura fi Arachnoid"},{"t":"Skin fi Supraspinous ligament fi Interspinous ligament fi Ligamentum flavum fi Arachnoid fi Dura"},{"t":"Skin fi Supraspinous ligament fi Ligamentum flavum fi Interspinous ligament fi Dura fi Arachnoid"},{"t":"Skin fi Supraspinous ligament fi Interspinous ligament fi Ligamentum flavum fi Dura fi Arachnoid"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Structures pierced while performing lumbar puncture: Skin fi Subcutaneous tissue fi Supraspinous ligament fi Interspinous ligament fi Ligamentum flavum fi Dura fi Arachnoid"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When Ligamentum flavum is pierced, it gives a popping or give way sensation."},{"k":"img","v":8223},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lumbar puncture:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Site of doing lumbar puncture: L3 & L4."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Position: A patient can sit or have lateral decubitus. A helper can assist the patient in sitting in a fetal position with knees up to the chest to access the back of the patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 types of needles: Traumatic: Quincke & Babcock . It is usually not preferred & associated with increased incidence of PDPH (Post dural puncture headache) Non-Traumatic: Sprotte & Whitacre"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Traumatic: Quincke & Babcock . It is usually not preferred & associated with increased incidence of PDPH (Post dural puncture headache)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-Traumatic: Sprotte & Whitacre"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Traumatic: Quincke & Babcock . It is usually not preferred & associated with increased incidence of PDPH (Post dural puncture headache)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-Traumatic: Sprotte & Whitacre"},{"k":"img","v":8226},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The needle has a double-edged bevel. The needle goes into the dura mater to separate the fibres."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The needle is introduced with the bevel facing up, progressing in a cephalad direction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the needle is taken out. It will evert the edges, leading to leakages and resulting in headaches."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contraindications: Raised ICP (papilledema) Focal neurological deficit (space occupying lesion) Altered mental status Bleeding diathesis Local site infection Kyphoscoliosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Raised ICP (papilledema)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focal neurological deficit (space occupying lesion)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered mental status"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bleeding diathesis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Local site infection"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kyphoscoliosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Raised ICP (papilledema)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focal neurological deficit (space occupying lesion)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Altered mental status"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bleeding diathesis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Local site infection"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kyphoscoliosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indications for Lumbar Puncture (CSF Tap): Diagnosis of Meningitis or Encephalitis Investigation of Subarachnoid Hemorrhage (SAH) Assessment of Inflammatory CNS Conditions : Multiple Sclerosis : Check for oligoclonal bands in the CSF (Diagnostic) Sarcoidosis : To assess for signs of CNS involvement. Cerebral Lupus Monitoring Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension (IIH) Detection of Malignancy: Perform cytology and detect malignant cells in cases of suspected metastatic cancer involving the CNS. Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) : For specific pathogen identification, such as for herpes simplex virus or tuberculosis . Antibody testing : To look for paraneoplastic antibodies or other immunological markers specific to CNS infections or autoimmune conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Meningitis or Encephalitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Investigation of Subarachnoid Hemorrhage (SAH)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Assessment of Inflammatory CNS Conditions : Multiple Sclerosis : Check for oligoclonal bands in the CSF (Diagnostic) Sarcoidosis : To assess for signs of CNS involvement. Cerebral Lupus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple Sclerosis : Check for oligoclonal bands in the CSF (Diagnostic)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis : To assess for signs of CNS involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral Lupus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitoring Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension (IIH)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detection of Malignancy: Perform cytology and detect malignant cells in cases of suspected metastatic cancer involving the CNS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) : For specific pathogen identification, such as for herpes simplex virus or tuberculosis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibody testing : To look for paraneoplastic antibodies or other immunological markers specific to CNS infections or autoimmune conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complications: Post lumbar puncture headache (30%) Secondary infections (rare)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post lumbar puncture headache (30%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary infections (rare)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis of Meningitis or Encephalitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Investigation of Subarachnoid Hemorrhage (SAH)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Assessment of Inflammatory CNS Conditions : Multiple Sclerosis : Check for oligoclonal bands in the CSF (Diagnostic) Sarcoidosis : To assess for signs of CNS involvement. Cerebral Lupus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple Sclerosis : Check for oligoclonal bands in the CSF (Diagnostic)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis : To assess for signs of CNS involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral Lupus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitoring Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension (IIH)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Detection of Malignancy: Perform cytology and detect malignant cells in cases of suspected metastatic cancer involving the CNS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) : For specific pathogen identification, such as for herpes simplex virus or tuberculosis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibody testing : To look for paraneoplastic antibodies or other immunological markers specific to CNS infections or autoimmune conditions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple Sclerosis : Check for oligoclonal bands in the CSF (Diagnostic)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sarcoidosis : To assess for signs of CNS involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral Lupus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post lumbar puncture headache (30%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary infections (rare)"}]},{"n":7,"q":"A patient undergoes lumbar puncture for the evaluation of suspected meningitis. Two days later, he develops a severe headache that worsens when he sits or stands and improves when lying down. He has no fever, neck stiffness, or neurological deficits. His headache persists despite conservative measures such as bed rest and analgesics. What is the next appropriate treatment?","o":[{"t":"Intravenous antibiotics"},{"t":"Blood patch"},{"t":"Steroid therapy"},{"t":"Acetaminophen"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Postdural puncture headache (PDPH) is a potential complication of a lumbar puncture, with symptoms caused by traction on pain-sensitive structures from low cerebrospinal fluid pressure (intracranial hypotension) following a leak of cerebrospinal fluid at the puncture site. Traumatic needles like Quincke, Babcock and also needles with lower gauge can cause PDPH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Definitive treatment with an epidural blood patch is recommended when symptoms persist even after conservative treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of PDPH:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bed rest for 8-12 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Analgesics"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous caffeine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Despite symptomatic treatment, if the headache persists: Low Volume Blood patch: For this procedure, 10 ml to 30 ml of autologous venous blood is injected by an anesthesiologist into the epidural space at the level of the prior procedure. This is thought to create a \"patch\" over the dural puncture and decrease CSF leak, or simply increase the intracranial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low Volume Blood patch: For this procedure, 10 ml to 30 ml of autologous venous blood is injected by an anesthesiologist into the epidural space at the level of the prior procedure. This is thought to create a \"patch\" over the dural puncture and decrease CSF leak, or simply increase the intracranial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low Volume Blood patch: For this procedure, 10 ml to 30 ml of autologous venous blood is injected by an anesthesiologist into the epidural space at the level of the prior procedure. This is thought to create a \"patch\" over the dural puncture and decrease CSF leak, or simply increase the intracranial pressure."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 38 y/o man presented to the ED with progressive weakness in his legs that started 4 days ago. He reports that the weakness began as difficulty climbing stairs and has now progressed to difficulty walking. He denies any trauma, fever, or sensory loss, although he has experienced some mild tingling in his fingertips. He reports no urinary or bowel problems. Which of the following best describes the CSF finding in this patient?","o":[{"t":"High protein with high white blood cell count"},{"t":"Low protein with normal white blood cell count"},{"t":"High protein with normal white blood cell count"},{"t":"Normal protein with high white blood cell count"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient presents with clinical features consistent with Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS), an autoimmune condition characterized by ascending weakness. The classic CSF finding in GBS is albuminocytological dissociation , which refers to elevated protein levels with a normal white blood cell count."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Pres Cells Sugar Protein Gross Appear sure ance"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Normal 50-2 0-4 >50-60% of <0.45g/l Clear"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"50 Lympho blood level mm cytes H2O"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Acute bacterial › > 1000 fl ›› Cloudy meningitis PMN"},{"k":"img","v":8235},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tuberculous › > 100-1 fl Clear/cloudy meningitis 000 A malnourished lymphoc child with PEM- ytes grade 4 per cubi BCG scar is c mm absent Contact with Koch's"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subarachnoid › RBC - Normal/› › Bloody in the haemorrhage predomi initial phase"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nantly after 48 hours."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Xanthochromi c CSF (due to hemolysis)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Viral meningitis Nor 10-2000 Normal Normal/› Clear"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"mal lymphoc"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ytes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"GBS Nor Normal Normal › Clear (Albumino mal cytological dissociation)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CNS leukemia & Cryptococcal meningitis: Sugar is low"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cobweb Coagulum: Clear fluid in a test tube with a cobweb at the bottom"}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 36 y/o patient presents with a 3-week history of headache, low-grade fever, malaise, and confusion. The patient is HIV-negative. O/E, neck rigidity and cranial nerve palsy are affecting the ocular nerves. Lumbar puncture shows elevated WBCs (mainly lymphocytes), high protein, and low glucose levels in the CSF. MRI reveals the following. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"Waiting for CSF culture results before initiating treatment"},{"t":"Initiating empirical anti-tuberculosis therapy immediately"},{"t":"Starting broad-spectrum antibiotics for bacterial meningitis"},{"t":"Performing a brain biopsy for a definitive diagnosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation (subacute onset with headache, cranial nerve palsies, and confusion), CSF findings (lymphocytic pleocytosis, high protein, low glucose), and MRI showing contrast-enhanced ring lesions are highly suggestive of tuberculous meningitis ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Immediate empirical anti-tuberculosis therapy is crucial to prevent irreversible neurological damage while awaiting culture results."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptoms and Clinical Headache , slight mental changes , and low-grade fever Features: can develop over weeks, often accompanied by malaise ,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"anorexia , and irritability . More severe cases present with confusion , lethargy , neck rigidity , and altered sensorium . Disease progression: evolves over 1–2 weeks, longer than bacterial meningitis. Cranial nerve palsies , especially ocular nerves, are common due to inflammation at the base of the brain. May result in coma , hydrocephalus , and intracranial hypertension if untreated. Cerebral ischemia can result from the involvement of cerebral arteries. Tuberculoma (space-occupying lesion) may cause seizures and focal neurological signs."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CSF Findings: Elevated leukocyte count (up to 1000/mL, predominantly"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lymphocytes). High protein content (1–8 g/L). Low glucose concentration ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigation of Choice: Culture of CSF (gold standard, takes 4–8 weeks,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"positive in ~50%). Smear for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) (low sensitivity, positive in 10–40%). PCR for Mycobacterium tuberculosis DNA (variable sensitivity)."},{"k":"img","v":8191},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Imaging:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment: Empirical therapy is often initiated based on suspicion."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Initial drug regimen : isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol, and pyridoxine. Duration : 6–12 months depending on response. Dexamethasone (12–16 mg/day) for 3 weeks, tapered for 3 more weeks (recommended for HIV-negative patients). Tuberculoma may require a biopsy for diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Waiting for CSF culture results before initiating treatment (Option A) is incorrect because waiting for culture results (which may take 4–8 weeks) could delay life-saving treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Starting broad-spectrum antibiotics for bacterial meningitis (Option C) is incorrect because the slower progression, CSF profile, and imaging findings favor tuberculous over bacterial meningitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Performing a brain biopsy for a definitive diagnosis (Option D) is not necessary initially as the clinical suspicion of tuberculous meningitis is high, and empirical treatment is warranted to prevent further neurological damage or death."}],"qi":[8191]},{"n":10,"q":"A 68 y/o immunocompromised patient with a history of CKD presents to the ER with fever, headache, and neck stiffness. Blood cultures are pending, and a lumbar puncture reveals elevated CSF protein, decreased glucose, and neutrophilic pleocytosis. Given the patient's age and comorbidities, which of the following is the most appropriate empirical antibiotic regimen?","o":[{"t":"Ceftriaxone and Vancomycin"},{"t":"Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone"},{"t":"Ampicillin, Ceftriaxone, and Vancomycin"},{"t":"Ceftazidime and Vancomycin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis in this scenario is bacterial meningitis , likely caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae or Listeria monocytogenes , based on the patient's advanced age, immunocompromised status, and CSF findings of elevated protein, low glucose, and neutrophilic pleocytosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The correct treatment includes Ampicillin for Listeria coverage in addition to Ceftriaxone and Vancomycin for broad-spectrum bacterial coverage including Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Requires immediate treatment; the goal is to start antibiotics within 60 minutes of hospital arrival."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Empirical Therapy: Initiated before CSF Gram's stain and culture results."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Age group Empiric Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Children <3 Months of Age Ampicillin : To cover Listeria monocytogenes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cefotaxime or Ceftriaxone : To cover S. pneumoniae , Group B streptococci , H. influenzae , and N. meningitidis Gentamicin : May be added in critically ill patients for synergy against Listeria monocytogenes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Children >3 Months to Adults (up to 55 years): Ceftriaxone or Cefotaxime : Covers S. pneumoniae ,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"N. meningitidis , H. influenzae Vancomycin : To cover cephalosporin-resistant S. pneumoniae Dexamethasone : To reduce meningeal inflammation Acyclovir : Added if HSV encephalitis is suspected Doxycycline : Added during tick season to cover tick-borne bacterial infections"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Adults >55 Years or Immunocompromised Patients Ampicillin : Added for Listeria monocytogenes coverage (including pregnancy, chronic illness, organ Ceftriaxone or Cefotaxime : To cover S. pneumoniae , transplantation, malignancy, or immunosuppressive N. meningitidis therapy): Vancomycin : To cover resistant S. pneumoniae"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patients with Pseudomonas aeruginosa Infection: Ceftazidime or Meropenem : For Pseudomonas"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"aeruginosa Vancomycin : Added in case of suspected Staphylococcal infections"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ceftriaxone and Vancomycin (Option A) and Ceftriaxone and Dexamethasone (Option B) are incomplete regimens since they do not address Listeria, which is critical in older or immunocompromised patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ceftazidime and Vancomycin (Option D) include Ceftazidime , which is used for Pseudomonas aeruginosa coverage and is unnecessary in this case unless the infection is suspected or proven."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 45 y/o patient presents with fever, headache, neck stiffness, and photophobia. On examination, the patient is lethargic and lumbar puncture reveals elevated CSF opening pressure, PMN leukocytosis, decreased glucose levels, and increased protein concentration. Which clinical sign is most likely to be present in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Kernig's sign"},{"t":"Tinel's sign"},{"t":"Positive Babinski sign"},{"t":"Positive Romberg sign"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of fever, headache, neck stiffness, photophobia, and altered consciousness, combined with CSF findings of PMN leukocytosis, low glucose, and elevated protein, is highly indicative of acute bacterial meningitis . Kernig's sign is commonly seen inbacterial meningitis, indicating meningeal irritation, which fits with the patient's symptoms andCSF findings."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Definition: Acute purulent infection in the subarachnoid space (SAS) leading to CNS inflammation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presentation: Can be acute and fulminant or subacute, with the classic triad of symptoms: Fever Headache Nuchal rigidity (stiff neck)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nuchal rigidity (stiff neck)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headache"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nuchal rigidity (stiff neck)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Consciousness: Decreased level of consciousness occurs in >75% of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cases, ranging from lethargy to coma."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Kernig's Sign: Positive when passive extension of the knee elicits pain;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"indicative of meningeal irritation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Brudzinski's Sign: Positive when passive flexion of the neck results in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"spontaneous flexion of the hips and knees; also indicative of meningeal irritation."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pupils: May present as dilated and poorly reactive due to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"increased intracranial pressure (ICP)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Posturing: Possible decerebrate posturing as a sign of severe CNS"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"dysfunction."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Signs of Increased Includes elevated CSF opening pressure (>180 ICP: mmH2O), papilledema, and potential cerebral herniation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(1–8% incidence)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Additional Nausea, vomiting, photophobia; seizures in 15–40% Symptoms: (focal or generalized)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Meningococcemia Erythematous maculopapular rash that quickly becomes Rash: petechial, commonly on the trunk and lower extremities."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Kernig & Brudzinski Sign:"},{"k":"img","v":8242},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tinel's sign (Option B): Associated with peripheral nerve irritation, not relevant to meningitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Positive Babinski sign (Option C): Indicates upper motor neuron dysfunction, which is not specific to meningitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Positive Romberg sign (Option D): Suggests proprioceptive dysfunction, usually seen in cerebellar or sensory issues, not bacterial meningitis."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 34-year-old male with untreated HIV presents with headache, fever, nausea, vomiting, and confusion. His CD4+ count is 45/mL. O/E, there is neck stiffness and altered mental status. Lumbar puncture shows elevated opening pressure, low glucose, and encapsulated yeast on India ink staining. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","o":[{"t":"High-dose oral fluconazole"},{"t":"IV acyclovir C"},{"t":"IV amphotericin B plus flucytosine"},{"t":"Oral corticosteroids plus broad-spectrum antibiotics"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is cryptococcal meningitis because the patient's clinical presentation, including HIV with low CD4+ count, elevated CSF opening pressure, and the identification of encapsulated yeast in the CSF via India ink stain, is characteristic of Cryptococcus neoformans infection."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"IV amphotericin B plus flucytosine is the standard initial treatment for cryptococcal meningitis to reduce fungal load and manage CNS infection."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Cause The leading infectious cause of meningitis in AIDS"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"patients is primarily due to Cryptococcus neoformans (C. neoformans); C. gattii accounts for up to 12%."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Presentat Symptoms include fever, nausea, vomiting, altered ion mental status, headache, and meningeal signs."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Low incidence of seizures and focal neurological deficits."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CSF Profile It may be normal or show modest elevations in WBC"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and protein levels. Decreased glucose levels may be observed. Elevated CSF opening pressure is typical."},{"k":"img","v":8248},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Identification of organisms via India ink examination of CSF."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Detection of cryptococcal antigen in CSF or blood. ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Induction therapy IV amphotericin B (0.7 mg/kg daily) or liposomal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"amphotericin (4–6 mg/kg daily) plus flucytosine (25 mg/kg qid) for at least 2 weeks. Monitor renal function to avoid flucytosine toxicity."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Maintenance Amphotericin should be continued until CSF culture is Therapy negative, followed by fluconazole (400 mg/d PO for 8"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"weeks, then 200 mg/d) until CD4+ T-cell count >200 cells/mL for 6 months."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"High-dose oral fluconazole (Option A): Used for maintenance therapy after initial treatment, not the first-line for acute management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"IV acyclovir (Option B): Appropriate for suspected viral encephalitis (e.g., HSV), not fungal infections like cryptococcal meningitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Oral corticosteroids plus broad-spectrum antibiotics (Option D): Used in conditions like bacterial meningitis with possibly raised intracranial pressure but not suitable for fungal meningitis."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 32 y/o HIV-positive patient with a CD4+ count of 50/mL presents with fever, headache, nausea, and altered mental status. A lumbar puncture is performed, and the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) shows elevated opening pressure. A specific staining test and MRI of the brain reveal the following. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Tuberculous meningitis"},{"t":"Cryptococcal meningitis"},{"t":"Cytomegalovirus encephalitis"},{"t":"Toxoplasmosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"India Ink Stain Findings: The encapsulated yeast cells seen in the CSF on India ink stain are hallmark features of Cryptococcus neoformans ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI Findings: The \"soap bubble\" appearance on MRI is characteristic of cryptococcal infection"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This combination of diagnostic findings—India ink stain and MRI imaging with a \"soap bubble\" appearance—is highly suggestive of Cryptococcus species, specifically C. neoformans , particularly in immunocompromised individuals like those with AIDS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Staining Imaging"},{"k":"img","v":8248},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"meningitis MRI\" data-author=\"Weerakkody Y, Sharma R, Kumar K\" data-hash=\"9469\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://radiopaedia.org/articles/cns-cryptoc occosis-2\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"190\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nZyOT6fg1744385226.png\" width=\"169\" />"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"MRI showing “Soap bubble appearance” of C.Neoformans"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"meningitis GMS stain\" data-author=\"Milman, Tatyana & Mirani, Neena & Turbin, Roger.\" data-hash=\"9471\" data-license=\"CC BY NC 4.0\" data-source=\"https://www. researchgate.net/figure/Budding-yeast-organisms-stain- with-Gomori-methenamine-silver-stain-A-GMS-stain-high _fig2_26730355\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"262\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"8JPvftVT1744385361.png\" width=\"343\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberculous meningitis (Option A): Typically presents with a subacute onset, and CSF shows a lymphocytic pleocytosis, low glucose, and high protein; encapsulated yeast is not seen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytomegalovirus encephalitis (Option C): Primarily causes encephalitis or retinitis in AIDS patients, but does not show encapsulated yeast or “soap bubble” brain lesions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Toxoplasmosis (Option D): Usually presents with multiple ring-enhancing lesions on MRI, but does not involve encapsulated yeast or fungal elements in the CSF."}],"qi":[8198]},{"n":14,"q":"A 34 y/o man with a history of HIV/AIDS presents to the ED with complaints of a severe headache, fever, and confusion. His CD4+ T-cell count is 150 cells/mL.\nNeurological examination reveals right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking. An MRI of the patient is given below. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"Initiate treatment with sulfadiazine and pyrimethamine with leucovorin."},{"t":"Perform a brain biopsy to confirm the diagnosis."},{"t":"Order a chest X-ray to rule out tuberculosis."},{"t":"Start empiric treatment for bacterial meningitis."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's presentation is highly suggestive of cerebral toxoplasmosis , a common opportunistic infection in patients with advanced HIV/AIDS, especially with a CD4+ count <200 cells/mL. The MRI findings of multiple ring-enhancing lesions further support this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the appropriate initial management is to start treatment with sulfadiazine and pyrimethamine with leucovorin."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cerebral Toxoplas mosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About Toxoplasmosis is a common secondary CNS infection in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"AIDS patients , but its incidence is decreasing due to ART. Occurs typically when CD4+ T-cell counts are <200/mL . Often represents the reactivation of latent T. gondii tissue cysts. Screening for IgG antibodies to T. gondii is recommended at HIV diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Cerebral toxoplasmosis usually arises from reactivation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"of latent cysts. It is 10 times more common in seropositive patients than seronegative ones. Presents with fever, headache, and focal neurological deficits (e.g., seizures, hemiparesis, aphasia). Cerebral oedema may cause confusion, dementia, lethargy , and progressing to coma."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations MRI typically shows multiple lesions with ring"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"enhancement and surrounding oedema . If MRI is unavailable, double-dose contrast CT may be used. Brain biopsy is definitive but reserved for patients who fail 2-4 weeks of empiric therapy . (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8204},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Order a chest X-ray to rule out tuberculosis. (Option C): While TB is a concern in HIV patients, the acute neurological symptoms and MRI findings align more with cerebral toxoplasmosis. A chest X-ray is not the immediate priority here."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Start empiric treatment for bacterial meningitis. (Option D): Bacterial meningitis could present similarly, but the MRI findings of ring-enhancing lesions are not typical for meningitis. The strong indication of cerebral toxoplasmosis suggests treating that condition first."}],"qi":[8204]},{"n":15,"q":"A 42 y/o woman with a history of HIV/AIDS presents to the neurology clinic with progressive weakness in her right arm and difficulty walking over the past month. She also reports occasional blurred vision and trouble speaking. Her CD4+ T-cell count is 80 cells/mL, and her current ART has been suboptimal. An MRI of the patient is given below. Which of the following findings would be most indicative of the condition affecting this patient?","o":[{"t":"Presence of JC virus DNA in the CSF."},{"t":"Multiple enhancing lesions on MRI."},{"t":"Significant headache and fever."},{"t":"Rapid improvement of symptoms after starting a new ART regimen."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms, including progressive weakness in her right arm, difficulty walking over, blurred vision, and trouble speaking, along with a low CD4+ T-cell count and MRI findings of non-enhancing hyperintense lesions, are consistent with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) caused by the JC virus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of JC virus DNA in the CSF is a key diagnostic marker for PML, with high specificity."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is a"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"serious opportunistic CNS infection in AIDS patients, caused by the JC virus . JC virus is a human polyomavirus ; around 80% of adults have antibodies to it, but less than 10% of healthy adults show ongoing replication. PML is the only known clinical disease caused by the JC virus."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology PML lesions start as small foci of demyelination in the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"subcortical white matter , eventually coalescing. The cerebral hemispheres, cerebellum , and brainstem can be involved. Neurological deficits are multifocal and often protracted . Symptoms include ataxia, hemiparesis, visual defects, aphasia , and sensory deficits. Seizures occur in ~20% of patients. Headache, fever, nausea, and vomiting are uncommon and suggest other diagnoses. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Investigations MRI shows multiple, non-enhancing white matter lesions ,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"particularly in the occipital and parietal lobes . (Option B ruled out) Lesions appear hyperintense on T2-weighted images and hypointense on T1-weighted images . JC virus DNA in CSF has a diagnostic sensitivity of 76% and specificity close to 100% . Multifocal Leucoencephalopathy\" data-author=\"Radswiki T, Sharma R, Nassar I\" data-hash=\"9504\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"https://radiopaedia.org/ articles/progressive-multifocal-leukoencephalopathy\" data-tags=\"\" height=\"346\" src=\"https://image.prepladder. com/notes/6rE8Mi2loxH9wAD48Epv1744387259.png\" width=\"298\" />"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment No specific treatment exists for PML, but ART has"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"improved survival rates, with some patients surviving >15 years . Before ART, median survival was 3-6 months . ART can cause paradoxical worsening due to immune reconstitution syndrome (IRIS). (Option D ruled out) Only ~50% of patients show neurological improvement with ART. A CD4+ T-cell count >100/mL and maintaining an HIV viral load <500 copies/mL are associated with a favourable prognosis."}],"qi":[8210]},{"n":16,"q":"A 35-year-old woman is suspected of having myasthenia gravis. The neurologist decides to perform the Tensilon test using edrophonium bromide. Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding the Tensilon test?","o":[{"t":"The effects of the test last for about 10 minutes"},{"t":"It is a highly specific and sensitive test for myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Muscle function typically improves within 30 seconds of injection"},{"t":"Intravenous atropine is unnecessary during the procedure"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Edrophonium bromide causes a rapid improvement in muscle function within 30 seconds."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tensilon Test :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Previously used to diagnose myasthenia gravis; now less common."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drug Used : Edrophonium bromide (short-acting anticholinesterase)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Effect : Muscle function improves within 30 seconds, lasting 2-3 minutes. (Option A ruled out) (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Limitations : Lacks complete specificity and sensitivity. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Safety : Intravenous atropine should be on hand to manage bradycardia. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Assessment : Plan the evaluation, focusing on specific speech or limb movements for objectivity."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"8226":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8198":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8235":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8204":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8248":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8210":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8242":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8215":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8184":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8218":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8223":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8191":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});